codemap 9
tour feature-welcome
kind flow
author ai
group features/gui
note A cold human opens the window with no saved layout. The shot of this repo at that moment is the tabbed workspace: Tours, an open tour, References, the Console, and a save button, and no key chord. The palette is ctrl+p, but nothing on that screen says so, so a symbol or an action stays hidden until they already know the key. Adding a tour by hand is already possible, with + new tour, a name and a kind, + step, a click on the step, and save, but those controls sit on different tabs and nothing walks them in order. The palette, the new-tour button and the Console each do a piece of this. None of them is a first panel with no tabs that lists the actions with their chords, and none of them points at the real controls one step at a time. The manual-tour pain inventory is a separate backlog, not the design of this screen. The guide names a dead end only where a step would otherwise teach one: a note is still tour-note or step-note in the Console, and the index is the one the tours command prints, not the 1.1 numbers in the window. Closing the window drops unsaved work, so the last step is save. Three openings were run on the real window. The board is one panel and the dump is panels 0[], tab@Tour is absent, and the palette row shows ctrl+p from the registry, with save as ctrl+s and back as alt+left. Add a tour replaces that panel with the working layout and a bar that names the next real control. The bar moves to the next step only when that control has been used: the form opens, the tour count grows, a step is added, the target is the step, then save. A scripted run on the fixture creates startup-hand, adds main, saves, and the bar is gone. The coach opening puts that same bar on the tabbed workspace immediately, so the first screen still has tabs. The checklist opening is tabless, but it copies + new tour into the panel, says the real Tours list and Symbols and save are not there, and does not show the palette chord. The board is the opening that meets the first screen and still teaches the controls where they already live.

